================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING STATISTICS
================================================================================

WHAT ARE STATISTICS?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Statistics are values calculated from existing grocery data.

Examples:

• Number of grocery records

• Number purchased

• Number remaining

• Total planned cost

• Purchased cost

• Remaining cost

• Shopping completion percentage

• Average cost per grocery-list entry


================================================================================
STATISTICS USED IN THIS LESSON
================================================================================

+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| STATISTIC            | CALCULATION                                   |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| Total Items          | items.length                                  |
| Purchased Items      | purchased records count                       |
| Remaining Items      | total - purchased                             |
| Total Cost           | SUM(price × quantity)                         |
| Purchased Cost       | SUM(purchased price × quantity)               |
| Remaining Cost       | total cost - purchased cost                   |
| Completion           | purchased / total × 100                       |
| Remaining %          | 100 - completion, when list is nonempty       |
| Average Line Cost    | total cost / number of grocery records        |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
IMPORTANT: WHAT DOES "ITEM COUNT" MEAN?
================================================================================

Suppose:

Milk:

quantity = 2


Bread:

quantity = 1


Then:

items.length

=
2


This means:

TWO GROCERY-LIST RECORDS


It does NOT mean:

3 physical units


In this lesson:

"Total Items"

means:

NUMBER OF GROCERY-LIST ENTRIES


If you wanted total physical units instead:

items.reduce(
  (sum, item) =>
    sum + item.quantity,
  0
)


would be a different statistic.


================================================================================
TOTAL COST
================================================================================

Each item's line cost is:

price
 ×
quantity


Example:

Milk:

2 × $3.99

=
$7.98


The total grocery cost is:

SUM OF ALL LINE COSTS


================================================================================
PURCHASED COST
================================================================================

Only purchased records contribute.

Example:

Milk:

purchased = true


Milk cost:

$7.98


Bread:

purchased = false


Bread contributes:

$0.00


Purchased Cost:

$7.98


================================================================================
COMPLETION PERCENTAGE
================================================================================

Formula:

purchasedItems
      ÷
totalItems
      ×
100


Example:

1 purchased

out of:

3 total


Calculation:

1 / 3 × 100

=
33.333...


Rounded:

33%


================================================================================
EMPTY-LIST EDGE CASE
================================================================================

If:

totalItems = 0


we must NOT perform:

0 / 0


Instead:

percentageComplete = 0


For this application, when the list is empty:

percentageComplete:
0


percentageRemaining:
0


This avoids representing an empty grocery list as:

100% remaining


which would not be particularly useful.


================================================================================
PART 3: CALCULATING STATISTICS
================================================================================

Create a reusable utility function.


File:

src/utils/statsCalculator.js


export function calculateStats(
  items
) {

  // ============================================
  // COUNTS
  // ============================================

  const totalItems =
    items.length


  const purchasedItems =
    items.filter(
      item =>
        item.purchased
    ).length


  const remainingItems =
    totalItems -
    purchasedItems


  // ============================================
  // COSTS
  // ============================================

  const totalCost =
    items.reduce(
      (sum, item) =>

        sum +
        item.price *
        item.quantity,

      0
    )


  const purchasedCost =
    items.reduce(
      (sum, item) =>

        item.purchased
          ? sum +
            item.price *
            item.quantity
          : sum,

      0
    )


  const remainingCost =
    totalCost -
    purchasedCost


  // ============================================
  // PERCENTAGES
  // ============================================

  const percentageComplete =
    totalItems === 0
      ? 0
      : Math.round(
          (
            purchasedItems /
            totalItems
          ) * 100
        )


  const percentageRemaining =
    totalItems === 0
      ? 0
      : 100 -
        percentageComplete


  // ============================================
  // AVERAGE
  // ============================================

  const averageLineCost =
    totalItems === 0
      ? 0
      : totalCost /
        totalItems


  // ============================================
  // RETURN
  // ============================================

  return {

    totalItems,

    purchasedItems,

    remainingItems,

    totalCost,

    purchasedCost,

    remainingCost,

    percentageComplete,

    percentageRemaining,

    averageLineCost

  }
}


================================================================================
WHY RETURN NUMBERS INSTEAD OF STRINGS?
================================================================================

The original implementation used:

totalCost.toFixed(2)


inside the calculator.


Problem:

toFixed()

returns a:

STRING


Example:

const value =
  (7.98).toFixed(2)


value:

"7.98"


That makes the calculation layer mix:

DATA CALCULATION

with:

DISPLAY FORMATTING


Cleaner architecture:

calculateStats()
     ↓
Numbers
     ↓
Footer
     ↓
Format Numbers for Display


Therefore:

stats.totalCost

remains:

17.98


and Footer renders:

stats.totalCost.toFixed(2)


================================================================================
CALCULATION EXPLANATION
================================================================================

TOTAL ITEMS:

const totalItems =
  items.length


PURCHASED:

const purchasedItems =
  items.filter(
    item =>
      item.purchased
  ).length


REMAINING:

const remainingItems =
  totalItems -
  purchasedItems


TOTAL COST:

const totalCost =
  items.reduce(
    (sum, item) =>
      sum +
      item.price *
      item.quantity,
    0
  )


PURCHASED COST:

const purchasedCost =
  items.reduce(
    (sum, item) =>
      item.purchased
        ? sum +
          item.price *
          item.quantity
        : sum,
    0
  )


REMAINING COST:

const remainingCost =
  totalCost -
  purchasedCost


AVERAGE LINE COST:

const averageLineCost =
  totalItems === 0
    ? 0
    : totalCost /
      totalItems


================================================================================
WHY "AVERAGE LINE COST"?
================================================================================

Suppose:

Milk:

quantity = 2

price = $3.99

line cost = $7.98


Average calculation:

totalCost /
number of grocery records


This is NOT:

average price of every physical unit


Therefore:

Average Line Cost

is more precise than:

Average Item Cost


================================================================================
PURE FUNCTION PATTERN
================================================================================

calculateStats():

• Receives items

• Doesn't modify items

• Calculates values

• Returns an object


Pattern:

INPUT
 ↓
CALCULATION
 ↓
OUTPUT


No React state is needed inside this utility.


